# Your Profile — reels-to-email

> **This file is YOUR personal configuration.**
>
> The AI reads it to know:
> 1. What language to write in
> 2. How to write (tone, length, style)
> 3. Which topics matter to you (for "relevant to teach" tagging)
>
> Edit anything below. Save. The next reel you send will use the new profile.
>
> **Setup:** Copy this file to `config/profile.md` and edit it. Your real `profile.md` is git-ignored — only this `.example` is committed.

---

## Language

```
he
```

Set to `he` for Hebrew, `en` for English, or any other natural language name in English (e.g. `Spanish`, `Portuguese`).

---

## About You

Replace this block with 1-3 sentences describing yourself and what you do.

> Example: "I am a UX designer focused on AI-powered tools. I teach intro courses about design systems and like to share practical workflows with my audience."

---

## Explanation Style

How you want the AI to write the "explanation" part of the email.

- **Length:** 200-500 words (adjust to taste)
- **Tone:** Direct, no fluff — get to the point
- **Avoid:** Generic AI-speak like "in today's fast-paced world..."
- **Include:** Concrete examples, names of tools/people mentioned, exact steps if shown
- **Structure preference:** Plain paragraphs OR numbered steps OR bullets — your choice

---

## Relevance Topics

The AI tags each reel as **"relevant to teach"** or **"not relevant"** based on this section.
List what counts as "your area" — what you'd want to learn, share, or teach.

### Relevant to me

Replace these with your actual topics. Be specific.

- (Your topic 1 — e.g. "AI agents and automation pipelines")
- (Your topic 2 — e.g. "Design systems and component libraries")
- (Your topic 3 — e.g. "Indie hacker / solopreneur tactics")
- ... add as many as you want

### Not relevant

What should NOT be tagged as relevant.

- (e.g. "Pure motivational content")
- (e.g. "AI news without practical application")
- (e.g. "Topics outside my niche")

---

## Tagging Rule (when in doubt)

**Default: tag as relevant.** You'll decide for yourself when reading the email.

(You can change this — e.g. "When in doubt: not relevant" if you prefer fewer false positives.)

---

## Research Focus (optional)

Anything specific you want the web-research step to look for?

- (Example: "Always check if there's an open-source alternative")
- (Example: "Look for academic references when claims are scientific")
- (Leave blank if nothing specific)
